{"type": "result", "session_id": "20261018_134119_7b1923", "exit_code": 0, "text": "Source: challenge_corpus (corpus-level comparison of all matched pairs). Corpus: 57 Britannica documents, 55 Wikipedia documents; every match was made by the provisional_lexical method; frequency cosine 0.5254; 19.25% of Britannica tokens were unverified (noisy OCR), so OCR errors limit these counts. Counts are corpus aggregates over only entries with an accepted match; there are no per-entry page IDs at this level.\n\nWords whose frequency most distinguishes the Britannica vocabulary from the Wikipedia vocabulary (distinctive_britannica, ranked by z-score):\n\n| term | z | count_britannica | count_wikipedia |\n|------|-----|------------------|-----------------|\n| brit | 5.531 | 33 | 0 |\n| placed | 3.699 | 23 | 10 |\n| native | 3.52 | 24 | 14 |\n| eyes | 3.44 | 18 | 6 |\n| marked | 3.359 | 15 | 3 |\n| cells | 3.192 | 12 | 1 |\n| afterwards | 3.057 | 12 | 2 |\n| fine | 3.038 | 17 | 9 |\n| piece | 2.961 | 13 | 4 |\n| principal | 2.929 | 12 | 3 |\n| force | 2.904 | 21 | 18 |\n| abdomen | 2.889 | 10 | 1 |\n\nInterpretation: \"brit\" is the strongest discriminator (33 in Britannica, 0 in Wikipedia), but it is almost certainly an OCR fragment (e.g., a truncated \"British\"/\"Britannia\") rather than a standalone word — treat it as an artifact of noisy OCR, not a genuine lexical difference. The next strongest genuine discriminators are \"placed\" (23 vs 10), \"native\" (24 vs 14), and \"eyes\" (18 vs 6), consistent with Britannica entries containing more physical/descriptive and natural-history phrasing.\n\nFor contrast, the words most distinctive of Wikipedia are: esarhaddon (1 vs 168), energy (0 vs 144), heat (15 vs 296), huguenot (0 vs 132), buxton (2 vs 145), huguenots (1 vs 130), political (1 vs 127), comitas (0 vs 102), century (2 vs 117), aquinas (0 vs 92), work (5 vs 147), known (2 vs 113).", "tokens": {"input": 11467, "output": 2306, "total": 13773, "cache_read": 0, "cache_write"